\DOC LOCAL_MUL_P256K1_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_P256K1_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes an inlined modular-multiplication subroutine in an
{eventually}-style machine goal, advancing the symbolic state by one step and
recording the multiplication's result as a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
{LOCAL_MUL_P256K1_TAC} takes an {int}, an offset/index used to locate the code
block to execute, and a {string list} of three names: the first names the
variable introduced for the computed result, the other two name the inputs
whose stored values the result is expressed in terms of (the destination and
the two source operands of the multiplication). Applied to a goal whose
conclusion is an {eventually} statement about state variable {s_n}, it advances
the state variable in the conclusion and in all state-dependent assumptions to
its successor. The old program-counter assumption, the old {bytes_loaded}, the
register- and memory-read assumptions and the old accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
assumption are discharged and re-asserted at the new state; the {MAYCHANGE}
assumption is extended by the memory region written, and there are added a read
assumption for that region equating it to a freshly introduced result variable,
a new program-counter assumption, and a pure arithmetic assumption defining
that variable as the product of the two operand values reduced modulo the field
prime. Purely arithmetic and nonoverlapping assumptions are kept, though their
position in the assumption list may change. The outer postcondition of the
conclusion is unchanged.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s_n
   ========================================  LOCAL_MUL_P256K1_TAC i [dest; src1; src2]
   A' ?- eventually ... s_{n+1}
}
\noindent where {A'} is as described above.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the tactic shows only this single-subgoal
state-advancing behaviour, always on goals of this machine-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
